  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 PM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThe post frontal airmass from yesterday's system has continues to \nspill into the Northern Plains today. 850 mb temperatures have \ncooled to the negative single digits, resulting in near seasonable \nhighs in the 30s to low 40s. Breezy northwest winds persist, \nresulting in wind chills down to near to below freezing. With high \npressure pushing into the forecast area tonight, low temperatures \nwill fall to the teens to low 20s in a few spots. Although winds \nwill be much lighter through the night, wind chills down to the \nteens to single digits are expected along the Buffalo Ridge in \nsouthwest Minnesota. \n\nThe surface ridge will push through the forecast area throughout the \nday on Monday. This will keep highs down in the 30s across the area. \nLight winds are expected but winds will be just elevated enough to \nkeep wind chills near to below freezing for the day. Bundle up \nbefore leaving to go to work or school Monday morning! Monday night \nwill be a rinse and repeat of Sunday night with lows bottoming out \nin the teens to low 20s. \n\nA shortwave trough will dive through the Northern Plains late \nTuesday through Wednesday, delivering the next potential for light \nprecipitation, including the first very light snow of the season. \nBefore the wave arrives, warm air advection (WAA) will be \nstrengthening ahead of this wave. The WAA will push highs slightly \nhigher Tuesday afternoon, into the 30s to low 40s. The next chance \nfor precipitation, and very light snow, returns to the area Tuesday \nnight. The WAA will persist into Tuesday night while deeper \ndendritic forcing spreads across the forecast area. There remains \nsome uncertainty regarding low level saturation but think the \ndynamics will be strong enough to overcome this limitation. This is \nsupported by the ensembles as they have increased their probability \nfor a hundredth of an inch of QPF. Now up to a 40-60% chance. \nThermal profiles will be near to below freezing, allowing for a \nsmall mix of rain and snow during the initial onset before quickly \nswitching to all snow. \n\nSnow will persist into Wednesday morning before tapering off by the \nafternoon hours. Any snow that does accumulate will be very light, \nwith amounts likely (60-80% chance) below a quarter of an inch. \nTough to say where the most favorable area is at this time but this \nwill get refined over the next day or so. While this is not all that \nimpactful in terms of amounts, a fresh coating of very light snow \ncould result in some minor travel impacts for those traveling for \nThanksgiving. Wednesday afternoon will be much quieter with highs \nremaining in the 30s to low 40, potentially a shade bit colder \ndepending on how extensive the very light snow is. \n\nThe rest of the work week looks to be dry as large scale decent \noverspreads the Northern Plains. High temperatures will remain near \nto above seasonable with highs in the 30s, 40s, to potentially \nreaching up to 50F on Friday.\n\nSimilar temperatures look to persist into next weekend. Medium range \nguidance does suggest the potential for another shortwave trough to \npush through the region during this period of time. The specific \ndetails of this potential wave are uncertain so have continued with \na dry forecast at this time. Will keep an eye on this wave over the \nnext week.",
